{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"injurious-definable-badger","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nWhen an LLM call is blocked by a content policy — an AWS Bedrock Guardrail intervention (Converse `stopReason=guardrail_intervened`) or an Anthropic native refusal — pydantic-ai normalizes it to `finish_reason=content_filter` and rebar classifies it `ResolutionClass.CHANGE_INPUT` (non-retryable), which is the correct disposition. However, the operator-facing message rendered by `message_for()` is `RESOLUTION_MESSAGES[CHANGE_INPUT]` = \"The input was rejected (too large / malformed) — reduce or fix it and retry.\" That parenthetical misreports the failure mode: a guardrail/content-policy block is neither \"too large\" nor \"malformed\", so an operator is sent to the wrong remedy. Confirmed live against real Bedrock Guardrails.\n\n## Approach\nRefine the message, do NOT reclassify. `CHANGE_INPUT` remains the correct class (shared with genuine size cases: HTTP 413, context-length 400), so the exit-code/telemetry behavior is unchanged. Add an optional `finish_reason` keyword to `message_for(resolution_class, *, finish_reason=None)`; when `resolution_class == CHANGE_INPUT.value` AND `finish_reason == \"content_filter\"`, return a new `CONTENT_FILTER_MESSAGE` that names the real cause (a Bedrock Guardrail or the model's own content filter / refusal) and states it is not a size/format problem. Otherwise return the existing generic message. `finish_reason` is already available at all three render sites (on `LLMOutcome.diagnostic[\"finish_reason\"]`, and on persisted `coverage[\"diagnostic\"][\"finish_reason\"]`), so thread it through. Leave `check_response`'s raise text unchanged (already accurate).\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/failure.py` — add `CONTENT_FILTER_MESSAGE`; add optional `finish_reason` kwarg + branch to `message_for`.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py` — pass `finish_reason` at the two `message_for` call sites (from the outcome and from persisted coverage diagnostic).\n- `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` — pass `finish_reason` from `_outcome.diagnostic`.\n- `tests/unit/` — new unit test for `message_for` disambiguation.\n\n## Testing\n- `content_filter` + `CHANGE_INPUT` → returns the new content-filter/guardrail message (happy path).\n- A non-content_filter `CHANGE_INPUT` (e.g. a 413 payload-too-large outcome) → still returns the generic message (edge: shared class not over-broadened).\n- `finish_reason=None` / omitted → generic message (back-compat for the string-only caller).\n- `content_filter` with a NON-CHANGE_INPUT class → unaffected (generic lookup).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A guardrail/content-filter denial surfaces an operator message that names a content policy / guardrail and does NOT say \"too large / malformed\".\n- Genuine size/malformed `CHANGE_INPUT` cases keep their existing message.\n- `message_for` remains backward-compatible when called with only a resolution_class string.\n- The resolution_class, retryable flag, and exit-code mapping are unchanged (messaging-only fix).\n- Unit tests cover all four branches above and pass under `make lint` + `make typecheck`.","id":"21fc-a459-e298-4c31","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Content-filter/Guardrail denial reports misleading 'too large / malformed' operator message"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786141251097010001,"uuid":"e5f3ec43-7385-41af-94cd-fea2478b9374"}